 to end it  and according to news reports ingram  told the court that britney wanted a  chance to speak but  he had no idea what she would say during  the court hearing britney said that he  never told her she had a right to file a  petition to end the conservatorship  i was told i have to sit down and be  evaluated  again if i want to end the  conservatorship ma'am i didn't know i  could petition the conservatorship to it  and end it i'm sorry for my ignorance  but i honestly didn't know that  i don't feel like i should even be in  room with anyone  to offend me by trying to question my  capacity of intelligence whether i need  to be in this stupid conservatorship or  not  if this is true this could be a serious  ethical lapse because  just because the lawyer is appointed  doesn't mean that they  don't have all of the same ethical  obligations to any client to  zealously represent that client to the  best of their abilities and in the best  interests  of their client and there are other  holes in the record regarding ingram as  well  probate law allows the court to set the  lawyer's fee and to bill it to the  conservatives estate  and ingram right now is making ten  thousand dollars a week in fees as her  lawyer  yet neither he nor the court could  produce the order which established his  rate or allowed him to build that much  it's possible that he was making ten  thousand dollars a week  since 2008. all the while there are  reports that britney was given an  allowance of only two thousand dollars a  week all the while  her estate is worth north of 60 million  dollars  however at the same time britney says  she also has developed a good  relationship with ingram  actually i know i've had grown with a  personal relationship with sam my lawyer  i've been talking to him like three  times  um a week now we've kind of built a  relationship but i haven't really had  the opportunity by my  own self to actually  pan pick my own lawyer by myself  and i would like to be able to do that  it's theoretically possible based on the  size of the estate that these fees are  warranted  it's possible based on the amount of  work that he is doing that these fees  are warranted  but we just don't happen to know the  situation with ingram just isn't clear  but it certainly seems suspicious on its  face and what we're witnessing here  may be true confusion by brittany and  ingram about the lawyer's role  the nature of the conservatorship can  often blur the ethical lines between  advocate and guardian california law  requires lawyers to maintain  confidentiality and loyalty to their  clients and there's no exception for  lawyers appointed to represent  conservatives and in fact  california rejected the proposed rule of  professional conduct which would have  given lawyers some freedom to give their  own opinion about their clients with  diminished capacity for example san  diego lawyers ann rudolph and ralph  hughes  argued that the nature of  conservatorship encourages lawyers to  actually report on their clients to the  court  explaining what they feel is in the best  interest of their client  rather than advocating for what their  clients actually want  they say that this violates a lawyer's  ethical duty to keep attorney-client  communications confidential  and in their view this violates a  lawyer's ethical duty to zealously  advocate for their client a lawyer quote  cannot tell the court what the client  has said without the client's consent  cannot ignore the client's stated  desires and goals and can't advocate  something that the client does not want  so for example this could lead a lawyer  to using the best interest standard to  make decisions about the client's mental  capacity decisions that arguably the  lawyer isn't qualified to make  and in other words a court appointed  lawyer should be an advocate for what  the client wants  not act as a guardian ad litem who  substitutes their own judgment for the  client's wishes  and it's also important to recognize  that despite the we're here to help  attitude  conservatorships are inherently  adversarial they strip people of their  most fundamental rights  now most lawyers would say that the  nature of this process requires lawyers  to be zealous advocates but  in california there are court decisions  allowing lawyers to exercise their  independent judgment on crucial matters  impacting their conservative including  whether to withdraw  life support in cases permitting lawyers  to decide to pull the plug an appeals  court has emphasized that there isn't a  law requiring a court to get involved in  a conservative's decision  to literally withdraw life support this  raises another question  does the court get involved when other  fundamental rights are at stake  like reproduction well we sort of have  an example  in the case of britney spears because  choosing whether  and when to have children is a  fundamental constitutional right that  includes  the right of procreation sterilization  and abortion yet  coerce sterilization in the u.s has a  long and horrible legal history  in 1927 oliver wendell holmes one of the  most respected jurists of all time wrote  one of the most shocking supreme court  opinions ever in buck vs bell which  upheld virginia's eugenical  sterilization act  this law allowed the state to forcibly  sterilize carrie buck  a poor woman that the state had said  should not be able to have children  the opinion is infamous because holmes  wrote three generations of imbeciles  is enough kerry buck actually didn't  have any discernible disability but her  foster family initiated a court  proceeding against her  after their nephew sexually assaulted  her the case is a lesson on how easy it  could be to convince a court to take  away a person's freedom it's also a  lesson  in even respected jurists on the supreme  court  get things horribly horribly wrong and  after buck versus bell america  sterilized about 70  000 people and in this case 